045585 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284468 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284469 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284472 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284500 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284501 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284502 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284503 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284504 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284506 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284507 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284508 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284515 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284516 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284582 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284585 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121535 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284108 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124828 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{3}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 9 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}A(x - a)$,\n\nwhere\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} = 6$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $A$ is positive-definite\n3. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124828 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124828 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121541 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284028 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124829 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{3}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 3 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x - a)$.\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $f^{″}(a)$ are $λ_{1} =-2, λ_{2} =-\\sqrt{6}$ and $λ_{3} = \\sqrt{6}$, what type of point is $a$?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124829 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=\\sqrt{6},$ what type of point is $a?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124829 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-\\sqrt{6}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =\\sqrt{6}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121547 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283985 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124830 1071 "Given the sphere $x^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} + (z - 1)^{2} = 2$, what is the domain of $z = z(x, y)$?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124830 1071 "Given the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=2,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124830 1074 "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121553 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284258 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124831 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} =-1$, find the domain of $z = z(x, y)$." 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124831 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124831 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121571 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284279 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124834 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $((x + 1)^{2})/(4) - ((y - 4)^{2})/(2) = 1$?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124834 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124834 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(-1,4).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121589 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284309 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124837 1071 "Identify the surface $z^{2} + y^{2} - x^{2} = 1$." 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124837 1071 "Identify the surface ${z}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124837 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperboloid of one sheet whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $a=b=c=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121595 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284295 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124838 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=8x,\\,-\\infty <y<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124838 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n$z^{2} = 8x, - ∞ < y < ∞$." 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124838 1074 "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nThus, a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis and $p=2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121601 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284276 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124839 1071 "Identify the surface $2x^{2} - 4z^{2} - 4x - y - 16z - 12 = 0$." 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186124839 1071 "Identify the surface $2{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}-4x-y-16z-12=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186124839 1074 "First, we complete the square in the $x$ and $z$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4{z}^{2}-4x-y-16z-12 & =0 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x)-4({z}^{2}+4z)-y-12 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-1)^{2}-1]-4[(z+2)^{2}-4]-y-12 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-1)^{2}-2-4(z+2)^{2}+16-y-12 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-1)^{2}-4(z+2)^{2}-y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1)^{2}-4(z+2)^{2}-y+2 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-1)^{2}-4(z+2)^{2} & =y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1,2,-2).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284614 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284616 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284615 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284548 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284549 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284546 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284547 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284436 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284437 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284438 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284439 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284440 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284434 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284435 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284446 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284447 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284452 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284449 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284451 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284432 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284441 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284612 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126809 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186129557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284262 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129557 1071 "![](images/16/16e40e931919d695fe867f128827bc5351e390bb712009bfba130d195f5f35a2.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $10\\,kg$ and $8kg$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $5\\,m,$ find the distance between the fulcrum and $A.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186129557 1071 "![](images/16/16e40e931919d695fe867f128827bc5351e390bb712009bfba130d195f5f35a2.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $10kg$ and $8kg$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $5m$, find the distance between the fulcrum and $A$." 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186129557 1074 "Let $d$ be the distance between the fulcrum and $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6df23f8b7fd4202667daa23b1dc110bb1d546516fd37a2abd208b9c1c84b79c4.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-10\\cdot d=-10d\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ e