86045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198684 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f: R^{2} → R$, we have the following data:\n$f(1, 0)|= 3, \\mid f^{′}(1, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(1, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1, 2)$." 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198684 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198684 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & ≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(4) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194741 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284278 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198690 1071 "The maximum value of $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} + 4x_{2}^{2}$, when $9x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{2}^{2} = 36$, is $40$. Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198690 1071 "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $40.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198690 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194934 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284320 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198723 1071 "Let $I = [0, 2]⊂R$ and $X_{i} = {i} \\cdot [0, i]$. Which of the following represents $⋂_(i ∈ I)X_{i}$?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198723 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198723 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195145 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283971 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198755 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198755 1071 "$u_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u_{1} \\cdot u_{2} = 0$\n2. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n3. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthonormal" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198755 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195151 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284087 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198756 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186198756 1071 "$u_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, u_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u_{1} \\cdot u_{2} = 0$\n2. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n3. ${Q(u_{1}), Q(u_{2})}$ is orthonormal" 13194139533406 17592186045585 false] [17592186198756 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284600 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281693 1081 "{-2,-1,0,1,2}" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281693 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281693 1084 #uuid "0020c219-d93f-5966-bde9-6491d097c082" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281694 1081 "(\\pm 3,0)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281694 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281694 1084 #uuid "0038c158-55e4-54f1-88ca-383efd36482d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281695 1081 "11\\,880" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281695 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281695 1084 #uuid "0055bb57-ef47-5975-8d19-125af061d403" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281696 1081 "(0,7)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186281696 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [175921862816