n conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =8>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3-\\sqrt{2}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3+\\sqrt{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284246 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284429 1069 "q-163789" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284429 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284429 1072 #uuid "c598e2d2-d26a-59ea-a0e5-a7e53e350ac2" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284429 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284034 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284430 1069 "q-163791" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284430 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=-19$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284430 1072 #uuid "00b2158a-941e-5a2f-b68c-c230bee6df19" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284430 1074 "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284027 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284431 1069 "q-16380" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284431 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-10{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+20x+36y=64?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284431 1072 #uuid "16ed9c1a-a347-5813-b8d3-8841cf010a43" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284431 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+20x+36y & =64 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+36y-10{x}^{2}+20x & =64 \\\\ 9[{y}^{2}+4y]-10[{x}^{2}-2x] & =64 \\\\ 9[(y+2)^{2}-4]-10[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =64 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-36-10(x-1)^{2}+10 & =64 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-10(x-1)^{2} & =64+36-10 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-10(x-1)^{2} & =90 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{10}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(1,-2).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284182 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284432 1069 "q-163803" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284432 1071 "![](images/12/1292177200af79b36b05111607a33d0057d417c3e2f432de8439defb44e8928e.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $12\\,yd$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $A$ has mass $6\\,lb$ and is $4\\,yd$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $B.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284432 1072 #uuid "1f11568c-2ec5-5057-b318-49ce0284d696" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284432 1074 "Let $m$ be the mass of $B.$\nThe length of the rod is $12\\,yd$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is $4\\,yd.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is\n\n$$\n12-4=8\\,yd,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/97/97c638d214774f8517b2724aabf8c91a1cc6b543f1ae17c5ba632acbe1ec59ff.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-6\\cdot 4=-24\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $m\\cdot 8=8m\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24+8m & =0 \\\\ 8m & =24 \\\\ m & =3\\,lb.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284263 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284433 1069 "q-163812" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284433 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284433 1072 #uuid "4ce7de24-db57-5ead-8c61-93889fcd8ed1" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284433 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284118 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284434 1069 "q-163815" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284434 1071 "A circular plate of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $4\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $0.5\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284434 1072 #uuid "c38f99e9-7f1d-5aed-b19d-85896ba0f1c3" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284434 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}=16\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{8}{16\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =64\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $0.5\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =64+{0.5}^{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64+2 \\\\  & =66\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284040 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284435 1069 "q-163819" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284435 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of an annulus $D$ centered at $O$ with inner radius $1\\,\\text{m},$ outer radius $3\\,\\text{m},$ and mass density \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\n$$\n\n measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the ring passing through a point $2\\,\\text{m}$ from its center.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\int {t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=({t}^{2}-2t+2){e}^{t}+C\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284435 1072 #uuid "eb2d176d-940c-5302-ac37-36cb4b6cb097" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284435 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ the center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{{e}^{r}}{r}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{r}}{r}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{r}{|}_{1}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =({e}^{3}-e)\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi ({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{r}}{r}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}{e}^{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({r}^{2}-2r+2){e}^{r}{|}_{1}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(5{e}^{3}-e)\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi (5{e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =2\\pi (5{e}^{3}-e)+{2}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi ({e}^{3}-e) \\\\  & =2\\pi (9{e}^{3}-5e)\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284315 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284436 1069 "q-163820" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284436 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}.$ If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.75\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through a point on the lamina at a distance of $45\\,\\text{cm}$ from its center of mass?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284436 1072 #uuid "8c01ba7f-2294-5653-814b-18b555d68896" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284436 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the lines is $d=45\\,\\text{cm}=0.45\\,\\text{m}.$ Then, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =1.75+(0.45)^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1.75+0.81 \\\\  & =2.56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $2.56\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284374 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284437 1069 "q-163822" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284437 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}.$ If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $12.6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through a point on the lamina at a distance of $1.2\\,\\text{m}$ from its center of mass?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284437 1072 #uuid "c91a2d4f-72e1-5f81-a838-40fec3199d97" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284437 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the lines is $d=1.2\\,\\text{m}.$ Then, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =12.6+(1.2)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12.6+8.64 \\\\  & =21.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $21.24\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284073 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284438 1069 "q-163824" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284438 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}.$ If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $0.8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through a point on the lamina at a distance of $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ from its center of mass?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284438 1072 #uuid "e520815d-6d18-5172-b415-b773144ec27d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284438 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the lines is $d=1.5\\,\\text{m}.$ Then, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =0.8+(1.5)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.8+2.25 \\\\  & =3.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $3.05\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283982 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284439 1069 "q-163827" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284439 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ and center of mass $(3,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(1,-1)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284439 1072 #uuid "497514c6-9f46-53f8-8a1d-c918dd9dcd31" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284439 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(3,1)$ and the line through the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(3-1)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =2+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =2+6.4 \\\\  & =8.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $8.4\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284074 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284440 1069 "q-163829" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284440 1071 "Consider a plane lamina of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ and center of mass $(4,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $3.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(2,-1)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284440 1072 #uuid "e875642f-df78-59e1-9a48-79dd674e8c63" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284440 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(4,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-4)^{2}+(-1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =3.2+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3.2+64 \\\\  & =67.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $67.2\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284052 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284441 1069 "q-163831" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284441 1071 "![](images/08/088d73d81b8b1705f8ff329ff38b8cd289bd8ecbe48ffc4c0bd18c823635b418.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $8\\,kg$ and $4\\,kg$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $6\\,m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. Find the distance between the fulcrum and $B.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284441 1072 #uuid "c10bb201-45fa-581d-8adf-4704b5fd71be" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284441 1074 "Let ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}$ be the distances between the fulcrum and $A$ and $B$, respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31dd97f05b7dde8459bf79efbebcac082571c49282aeb63f926b0c1c058923c8.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-8\\cdot {d}_{1}=-8{d}_{1}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $4\\cdot {d}_{2}=4{d}_{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe principle of moments tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nSo, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n4{d}_{2}-8{d}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe need to find ${d}_{2},$ so we eliminate ${d}_{1}.$\nUsing the fact that the rod has length $6\\,m,$ we have\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}+{d}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{d}_{1}=6-{d}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{d}_{2}-8(6-{d}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4{d}_{2}-48+8{d}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 12{d}_{2} & =48 \\\\ {d}_{2} & =4\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284183 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284442 1069 "q-163838" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284442 1071 "![](images/78/784f2cbc652df83239e40dbe830675a4c3ce99c9f51dec913ff2ec1f62612cdc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284442 1072 #uuid "999c6d8f-f6eb-52c5-981d-e4dac50005ae" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284442 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y)=(-3,2).$\n\n![](images/39/39b694d4ea57e59d97102963009322aeef84d5fa58df07dbc854a73972ad7458.png)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284132 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284443 1069 "q-16396" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284443 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={y}^{2}x\\,,\\,y\\ne 0\\,.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284443 1072 #uuid "157e16d4-eb84-5272-a4e6-39b4e7ee7766" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284443 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284236 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284444 1069 "q-16399" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284444 1071 "![](images/9e/9e0782271a6fa3565b200e824f0dfca5645d4a197cf2703c7730e9345567befa.png)\n\nWhat complex number is represented in the Argand diagram above?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284444 1072 #uuid "23079d0b-b0fb-5485-9bb5-e3d922050d99" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284444 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(5,7).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=5,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=7.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=5+7\\text{i}.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =5+7\\text{i} \\\\  & =7\\text{i}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284149 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284445 1069 "q-16401" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284445 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284445 1072 #uuid "5ce4583e-c99f-532c-8f96-a07269c60d3f" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284445 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,y & ={{1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284193 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284446 1069 "q-164128" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284446 1071 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ about the perpendicular line passing through its center of mass is $4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nIf the moment of inertia of the same lamina about another perpendicular line is $7\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance between the two lines." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284446 1072 #uuid "d075cbf3-04ff-511a-a444-ca7a83f0ffb8" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284446 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nApplying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 7 & =4+6{d}^{2} \\\\ 6{d}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ d & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the two axes is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,m.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284256 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284447 1069 "q-164130" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284447 1071 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ about the perpendicular line passing through its center of mass is $5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nIf the moment of inertia of the same lamina about another perpendicular line is $\\frac{35}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance between the two lines." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284447 1072 #uuid "96575999-c780-5960-89f1-5ff2b6141012" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284447 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nApplying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =5+3{d}^{2} \\\\ 3{d}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ d & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the two axes is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\,m.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284289 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284448 1069 "q-164138" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284448 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284448 1072 #uuid "8eeb45f2-bc46-5681-a60d-c808242e288d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284448 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284050 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284449 1069 "q-164139" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284449 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-3\\le y\\le 3},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284449 1072 #uuid "e3b63dd6-0039-55fc-b091-4b9b21e853da" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284449 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 6=12\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{5})}{12}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{-3+3}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSince the center of mass is at the origin, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=-3}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{x}^{2}+18\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[2{x}^{3}+18x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(4+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =4+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =4+6 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284158 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284450 1069 "q-16414" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284450 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=5-12\\text{i}?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284450 1072 #uuid "f33a661d-acf6-551f-b7e5-6ad7d3cf9b2f" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284450 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=5-12\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $-12\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $12\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=5+12\\text{i}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284211 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284451 1069 "q-164140" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284451 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284451 1072 #uuid "0a041688-e59e-573f-8208-e1c91f1707b9" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284451 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=4\\cdot 2=8\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-2+2}{2},\\frac{-1+1}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSince the center of mass is at the origin, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{32}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{40}{3}) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =5+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284224 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284452 1069 "q-164149" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284452 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=4xy,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. The moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $\\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nGiven that the center of mass of the plate is at $(2,\\frac{2}{3}),$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284452 1072 #uuid "332d8074-30b5-5538-8a60-f4563af33aa4" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284452 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =9\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,\\frac{2}{3})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}((x-2)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2})\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x(x-2)^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}x(x-2)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{9}x{y}^{3}+\\frac{2}{9}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}x(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{4}{9}x+\\frac{2}{9}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{73}{36}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{73}{72}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{81}{8}-18+\\frac{73}{8}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{16}{3} & =5+9{d}^{2} \\\\ 9{d}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ d & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284117 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284453 1069 "q-164150" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284453 1071 "![](images/fd/fdfae6a5a321d1dbc235d06438847d5a0cc471fa4e31575f36c3967dca239824.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284453 1072 #uuid "140468c2-8315-56a7-adfc-2601c4482a06" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284453 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/82/82291f89bd32f3a3a20d39dfc89de91b2f673bec7c802cebf6d5fa3b4136ace1.png)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284091 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284454 1069 "q-164162" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284454 1071 "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284454 1072 #uuid "4e8234e3-2169-51fc-89b1-da45a80005bb" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284454 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $C.$ Hence, $B$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $B({x}_{B},{y}_{B},{z}_{B}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{B},{y}_{B})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $B$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/6d/6d98be34c848e0fbce571fc572fe9dae244e6ce80b677063fea2e19983e1a2da.png)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284126 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284455 1069 "q-164164" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284455 1071 "![](images/9b/9bafb9e41a34d03b70ca2b06bbb8c498f12b51f6e1625ddcd3a95fc4edbbccb6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284455 1072 #uuid "03114989-c6d6-500f-a868-4142afdca16c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284455 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/b5/b5e614ba1e002bef688b899e9f837ed3199748f02dd41e822340219b0201c492.png)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284269 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284456 1069 "q-164169" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284456 1071 "![](images/86/867fa59781c1732536e984398478996150c3d6b715c9d216bb82a35e0caf27e0.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284456 1072 #uuid "25f6d53a-4346-557b-a7bc-14685aa9ba47" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284456 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,1),$ so the global minimum point is $(1,1,-2).$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2c084b2697ada9a957eec3629ac71aea150563a3af37b4f5d0b25e1921559a.png)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284134 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284457 1069 "q-164171" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284457 1071 "![](images/78/78c28583961f2588c3e080d227e2e4f20a557ed77dcb20a6332092fe5915b22c.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284457 1072 #uuid "89ef4ae2-d60c-5f6f-8b7b-82204e9a3ac0" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284457 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2)=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=5\\cdot 7-3\\cdot 9=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,8\\,|\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284297 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284458 1069 "q-164175" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284458 1071 "Approximate the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.1060$ using interval bisection.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ | $\\,0.0869\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ | $\\,0.1038\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ | $\\,0.1230\\,$ |" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284458 1072 #uuid "783fc9ad-2dab-538a-ae36-a5d51bb5c0a3" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284458 1074 "First, we find the two numbers in the table that $0.1060$ lies between. These numbers are $0.1056$ and $0.1075.$ So, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1056 & <0.1060<0.1075\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above inequality in terms of $Φ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.25) & <Φ(z)<Φ(-1.24).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using interval bisection, we can approximate $z$ as\n\n$$\nz≈\\frac{-1.25+(-1.24)}{2}=-1.245.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284287 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284459 1069 "q-16419" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284459 1071 "If $z=5+5\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284459 1072 #uuid "ffd9d977-ab54-5f18-98fb-c284e3e5b28b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284459 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=5-5\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(5+5\\text{i})+(5-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(5+5)+\\text{i}(5-5) \\\\  & =10+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284039 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284460 1069 "q-164216" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284460 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284460 1072 #uuid "34ac06c6-738c-5077-b953-6f19c9bf7aa1" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284460 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipsoid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284292 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284461 1069 "q-16423" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284461 1071 "Given that $z=4+9\\text{i}$, calculate $z\\overset{―}{z}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284461 1072 #uuid "c4891bd7-301f-542e-bf98-c65b22eb754e" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284461 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=4+9\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=4,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=9,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =16+81 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=4+9\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=4-9\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(4+9\\text{i})(4-9\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+(4)(-9)\\text{i}+(4)(9)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(9)^{2} \\\\  & =16-36\\text{i}+36\\text{i}-(-1)(81) \\\\  & =16+81 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284330 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284462 1069 "q-164265" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284462 1071 "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $3z+6=3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284462 1072 #uuid "2cb8c0a4-a2ed-5b52-8a78-e703db55698d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284462 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+6 & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ 3(z+2) & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z+2 & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(3,-2,-2).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284092 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284463 1069 "q-164269" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284463 1071 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284463 1072 #uuid "03b953d0-f6a1-54cc-956c-27178aec298e" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284463 1074 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nx-0=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-(-1))^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $x$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,3,-1).$\n Since it is oriented along the $x$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284192 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284464 1069 "q-164270" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284464 1071 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284464 1072 #uuid "7722e4d9-5bec-545d-832c-cb50e7715d46" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284464 1074 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\ny-(-1)=\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $y$-axis that has its vertex at $(-4,-1,2).$\n Since it is oriented along the $y$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284208 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284465 1069 "q-164273" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284465 1071 "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284465 1072 #uuid "4f605c5a-bdb8-58e6-a4e2-fd64274d89f4" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284465 1074 "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+4 & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(3,-1,-4).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284344 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284466 1069 "q-164275" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284466 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284466 1072 #uuid "977b790d-d528-5cb6-b881-449f5c0f1471" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284466 1074 "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-4$ and $z=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284249 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284467 1069 "q-164276" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284467 1071 "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $2(x-5)^{2}-3(y-1)^{2}=z+5$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284467 1072 #uuid "1c4e75ca-d0de-5c84-afaa-4eb1e04c61e1" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284467 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5)^{2}-3(0-1)^{2} & =0+5 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-3 & =5 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=3$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284081 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284468 1069 "q-164277" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284468 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2}=-1,$ find its $y$-intercepts." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284468 1072 #uuid "bf26d7e4-1a7e-56ee-a907-48a28ff7ba03" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284468 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(0-3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 4+{y}^{2}-9 & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,2,0).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284015 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284469 1069 "q-164278" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284469 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1,$ find its $x$-intercepts." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284469 1072 #uuid "9b3e714a-6331-583d-877a-3a1b6b241c29" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284469 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5} & =-1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the hyperboloid does not intersect the $x$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284298 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284470 1069 "q-164279" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284470 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+2=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3}?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284470 1072 #uuid "7539eac9-bac1-5e13-b04d-8ad08919bed4" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284470 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+2 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z+2 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ z+2 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3 \\\\ z+5 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2(z+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $xz$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284167 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284471 1069 "q-164282" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284471 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8$ and the plane \n$z=4$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284471 1072 #uuid "c4fbc092-8a05-5e53-ba3e-bf69c3dd36b6" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284471 1074 "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=4$, we substitute $z=4$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=4+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284231 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284472 1069 "q-164284" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284472 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-(z-1)^{2}=-1,$ find its $y$-intercepts." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284472 1072 #uuid "87f4b219-b4ca-56b4-a84e-4618201b700e" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284472 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-(0-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-1 & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5} & =-3 \\\\ (y+6)^{2} & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the hyperboloid does not intersect the $y$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284025 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284473 1069 "q-164303" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284473 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284473 1072 #uuid "4ebd90dc-fabd-5a9a-8a63-d3d14e481ecf" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284473 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284366 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284474 1069 "q-164305" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284474 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284474 1072 #uuid "5e42866d-b20f-5a25-a416-745497c6d775" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284474 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283959 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284475 1069 "q-164307" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284475 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2}=1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284475 1072 #uuid "cc95e142-b514-5431-9924-bdc1db9331dd" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284475 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-(z-3)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =(z-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-3)^{2} & =(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\ z & =3\\pm \\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the circle $(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1}.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283969 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284476 1069 "q-16431" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284476 1071 "if ${z}_{1}=5+5\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=-3+8\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284476 1072 #uuid "703d20d4-bd30-5d37-b504-cb16788409d3" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284476 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5+5\\text{i}}{-3+8\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $-3+8\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-3-8\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-3-8\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{5+5\\text{i}}{-3+8\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+5\\text{i}}{-3+8\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-3-8\\text{i}}{-3-8\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15-40\\text{i}-15\\text{i}-40{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-{8}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15-55\\text{i}-40(-1)}{{3}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15-55\\text{i}+40}{9+64} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-55\\text{i}}{73}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25-55\\text{i}}{73}=\\frac{25}{73}-\\frac{55}{73}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284380 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284477 1069 "q-164310" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284477 1071 "Given the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=5,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284477 1072 #uuid "23c592cc-cea2-59e9-87a3-1ede25e122f0" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284477 1074 "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =5-((x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}) \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{5-((x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-((x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(2,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284363 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284478 1069 "q-164317" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284478 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284478 1072 #uuid "52e8bd9d-8ee3-5485-a8a6-bac786018ef0" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284478 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}=1$ and its boundary. \n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284057 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284479 1069 "q-16433" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284479 1071 "$\\frac{9\\text{i}}{-5+4\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284479 1072 #uuid "2e45dca1-bb14-5a43-81c1-494f270d1180" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284479 1074 "The denominator is $-5+4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-5-4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-5-4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\text{i}}{-5+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{9\\text{i}}{-5+4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-5-4\\text{i}}{-5-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-45\\text{i}-36{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{5}^{2}-{4}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-45\\text{i}-36(-1)}{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-45\\text{i}}{25+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-45\\text{i}}{41}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36-45\\text{i}}{41}=\\frac{36}{41}-\\frac{45}{41}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284218 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284480 1069 "q-164333" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284480 1071 "The table below gives the values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z>z)=0.01.$\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284480 1072 #uuid "85c72120-a302-5186-9506-8f0d0fa22ef4" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284480 1074 "First, we express the given information in terms of $Φ,$ using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>z) & =0.01 \\\\ 1-P(Z\\le z) & =0.01 \\\\ 1-Φ(z) & =0.01 \\\\ Φ(z) & =1-0.01 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.99).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.99,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np=0.99\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(p)=2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)=2.3263.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284172 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284481 1069 "q-16436" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284481 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284481 1072 #uuid "04eeeae4-c27a-580c-95e7-3112d95f585b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284481 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{2},\\,h=-1,\\,k=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1) & =\\pm (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}})(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284286 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284482 1069 "q-164377" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284482 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=-1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284482 1072 #uuid "4c89427b-fc60-5717-8e73-c405cf88965d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284482 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperboloid. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a hyperbola in $yz$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284307 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284483 1069 "q-164378" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284483 1071 "Given the sphere $(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284483 1072 #uuid "6afd7b69-5cc1-5a73-9071-703e04661638" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284483 1074 "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-4)^{2} & =9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =4\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284047 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284484 1069 "q-164379" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284484 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12}=-1$ and the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284484 1072 #uuid "dffb042f-0149-507c-b07f-f0b0026b0640" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284484 1074 "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $x=\\sqrt{2},$ we substitute $x=\\sqrt{2}$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =-1 \\\\ 2-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $6.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283960 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284485 1069 "q-164380" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284485 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(z-2)^{2}=-1$ and the plane $z=3$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284485 1072 #uuid "b6cf5542-c57d-51eb-9c7d-5efe33d9469c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284485 1074 "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z=3,$ we substitute $z=3$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(3-2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+1 & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $6$ and $2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284239 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284486 1069 "q-164397" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284486 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284486 1072 #uuid "ed51767d-9824-5d39-b6b6-e61b0eec9664" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284486 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ 8-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7} & =7 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =49 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 7 \\\\ y & =-1\\pm 7 \\\\ y & =-8,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $y=-8$ and $y=6$ in the $yz$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283945 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284487 1069 "q-164398" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284487 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284487 1072 #uuid "afb60cb3-06a3-5237-8d36-2a300505f7f2" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284487 1074 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $x=1$ and $x=5$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284382 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284488 1069 "q-16440" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284488 1071 "$\\frac{5}{-2+4\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284488 1072 #uuid "ad40aed2-4d3b-56e9-91e7-69a3bcf14434" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284488 1074 "The denominator is $-2+4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-2-4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-2-4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{-2+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5}{-2+4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-2-4\\text{i}}{-2-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10-20\\text{i}}{4+8\\text{i}-8\\text{i}-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10-20\\text{i}}{4-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10-20\\text{i}}{4-16(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10-20\\text{i}}{4+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10-20\\text{i}}{20}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-10-20\\text{i}}{20}=-\\frac{10}{20}-\\frac{20}{20}\\text{i}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284237 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284489 1069 "q-164408" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284489 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284489 1072 #uuid "23a6cffd-2f3e-55b9-90d1-e96cd073fd8e" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284489 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3(1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x-1)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284041 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284490 1069 "q-164411" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284490 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic cylinder $3(x+1)^{2}-6(z-2)^{2}=12$ and the plane $y=1$ onto the $xz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284490 1072 #uuid "a62eeaa1-08b2-5837-81a7-678acd38fe82" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284490 1074 "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $y=1,$ we substitute $y=1$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $y$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+1)^{2}-6(z-2)^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{(2)^{2}}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $2$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284179 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284491 1069 "q-164414" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284491 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the parabolic cylinder $4(x-3)^{2}=12(z+1)$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of the parabola?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284491 1072 #uuid "d45d5923-cf19-557a-b6bb-276b79a4af56" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284491 1074 "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $y=2,$ we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $y$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2} & =12(z+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3(z+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (z+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane with the vertex at $(3,-1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284310 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284492 1069 "q-164415" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284492 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic cylinder $2(x+6)^{2}-5{y}^{2}=10$ and the plane $z=5$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284492 1072 #uuid "f9fe7c1c-a0c0-5194-ad34-b26b4e46d994" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284492 1074 "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $z=5,$ we substitute $z=5$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $z$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+6)^{2}-5{y}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284000 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284493 1069 "q-164418" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284493 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284493 1072 #uuid "9ff5d288-247e-5774-82fd-1ca9271248d5" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284493 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(3,-6).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284037 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284494 1069 "q-164419" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284494 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(x-1)^{2}=3y?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284494 1072 #uuid "6b95d118-b433-55d2-98f9-256c4ab46ffd" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284494 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our parabolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}=3y,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x-1)^{2}=3y}.\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284104 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284495 1069 "q-164425" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284495 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284495 1072 #uuid "12ff0311-caaa-5591-a831-5519fc8ddee1" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284495 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(0,3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284181 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284496 1069 "q-164463" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284496 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284496 1072 #uuid "31fcd20a-8fd5-597c-a532-4ac405122b14" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284496 1074 "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283984 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284497 1069 "q-164464" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284497 1071 "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284497 1072 #uuid "abae05d9-082f-517b-a125-5470f720b0da" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284497 1074 "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283952 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284498 1069 "q-164465" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284498 1071 "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3y={z}^{2}-9{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $f?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284498 1072 #uuid "e71830d6-68be-53b3-b5cf-367d04008833" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284498 1074 "Solving our equation for $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y={z}^{2}-9{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284322 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284499 1069 "q-164469" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284499 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{2z}{3}=4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284499 1072 #uuid "0841a3ac-0926-5cd6-90a6-7052d8091dc6" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284499 1074 "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{2z}{3}=4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$   \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284277 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284500 1069 "q-164474" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284500 1071 "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}-(z-1)^{2}=1\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284500 1072 #uuid "d1da5d09-9bba-571b-a103-055c209c6aeb" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284500 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperboloid of one sheet with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(-1,3,1),\\,a=b=c=1.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283943 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284501 1069 "q-164480" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284501 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=8y,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284501 1072 #uuid "1c75c1ea-55b3-574e-8866-696724a9a0aa" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284501 1074 "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder, whose axis is the $z$-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a parabolic cylinder with $p=2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284240 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284502 1069 "q-164482" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284502 1071 "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}=-y,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty \n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284502 1072 #uuid "e7fed2b4-bf9a-591c-9b0c-e5b5d111888d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284502 1074 "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder, whose axis is the $z$-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nand a parabolic cylinder, shifted ${x}_{0}$ along the x-axis and ${y}_{0}$ along the y-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}=4p(y-{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a parabolic cylinder with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(-2,0),\\,p=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284264 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284503 1069 "q-164507" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284503 1071 "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284503 1072 #uuid "a1d6075c-8ace-59ee-87a9-ea3dfc142977" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284503 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperboloid of one sheet whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis and $a=1,\\,b=\\frac{1}{2},\\,c=3.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284235 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284504 1069 "q-164508" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284504 1071 "Identify the surface $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=-1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284504 1072 #uuid "e5b08a4f-9048-55ee-bcba-020f5d7e830c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284504 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperboloid of two sheets whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $a=b=1,\\,c=3.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284105 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284505 1069 "q-16451" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284505 1071 "![](images/61/6116ecf00a26d9828b238879b61385c1296f33ff863dbb8337a3b22be5981f10.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284505 1072 #uuid "fdeb0ff5-45e6-5343-a0cd-f339e82d372b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284505 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283955 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284506 1069 "q-164510" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284506 1071 "Identify the surface $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284506 1072 #uuid "e28adc4e-c42a-55a7-ab38-db39626f07cf" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284506 1074 "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $b=\\sqrt{2},\\,c=2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284202 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284507 1069 "q-164514" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284507 1071 "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284507 1072 #uuid "f0fa11d0-631b-5a1c-a346-e7803210c43a" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284507 1074 "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cone centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic cone centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic cone whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $a=1,\\,b=c=2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284177 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284508 1069 "q-164516" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284508 1071 "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1,\\,-\\infty <x<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284508 1072 #uuid "555cf7a1-a40b-509e-b58a-e7cfea474b5b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284508 1074 "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $b=c=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284148 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284509 1069 "q-16452" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284509 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284509 1072 #uuid "fc47bd40-4608-5f54-a026-2eadf6dc5b59" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284509 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284146 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284510 1069 "q-164529" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284510 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284510 1072 #uuid "9b1dbe1a-4744-5d2b-b075-9b0768e44a32" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284510 1074 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\sqrt{3}Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284296 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284511 1069 "q-164536" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284511 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284511 1072 #uuid "3f2fde67-bcf7-53c3-b2cf-283192588e86" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284511 1074 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284226 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284512 1069 "q-164538" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284512 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nparametrizes the elliptic 