ic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284513 1072 #uuid "d47c9c2f-b85d-5864-9454-5cf822acfda9" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284513 1074 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,3X,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284014 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284514 1069 "q-16454" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284514 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284514 1072 #uuid "2a341dec-953c-5c37-b7a1-a5cd95c6de7d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284514 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284090 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284515 1069 "q-164543" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284515 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}-3{z}^{2}=1,\\,-\\infty <x<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284515 1072 #uuid "8f6408ec-2d9a-573b-91f2-4270763372c3" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284515 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperbolic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperbolic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $b=\\frac{1}{2},\\,c=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283949 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284516 1069 "q-164548" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284516 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=-12y,\\,-\\infty <x<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284516 1072 #uuid "fe349944-268d-5340-92ca-d28764061da5" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284516 1074 "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nThus, a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis and $p=-3.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284388 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284517 1069 "q-164549" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284517 1071 "Identify the surface ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x-4y-6z-4=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284517 1072 #uuid "1b66372b-0da7-5bff-8ad5-ecdf16f5fac5" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284517 1074 "First, we complete the square in the $y$ and $z$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x-4y-6z-4 & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-4y)-({z}^{2}+6z)-x-4 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4-(z+3)^{2}+9-x-4 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-(z+3)^{2}+1-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2}-(z+3)^{2}+1-x & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-(z+3)^{2} & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1,2,-3).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284359 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284518 1069 "q-164550" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284518 1071 "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-2y-8z-9=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284518 1072 #uuid "2297e678-9e2f-50f7-bf32-a9e17f605ed6" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284518 1074 "First, we complete the square in the $y$ and $z$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-2y-8z-9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-({y}^{2}+2y)+2({z}^{2}-4z)-9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+1)^{2}+1+2[(z-2)^{2}-4]-9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+1)^{2}+1+2(z-2)^{2}-8-9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+1)^{2}+2(z-2)^{2}-16 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(y+1)^{2}+2(z-2)^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+1)^{2}+2(z-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $(0,-1,2).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284188 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284519 1069 "q-164570" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284519 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284519 1072 #uuid "7653a746-08d6-57ac-b90a-90dc212f031b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284519 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283968 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284520 1069 "q-164571" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284520 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25.$ By expressing the equ