 point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,3Z,\\sqrt{3}\\,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3Z,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284136 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284523 1069 "q-164581" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284523 1071 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\