c{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =11+\\frac{1}{2}(44) \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283972 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284531 1069 "q-164833" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284531 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284531 1072 #uuid "854e79dd-e49f-5d57-896b-a1669e5ffce7" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284531 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284300 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284532 1069 "q-164834" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284532 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2t-1⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284532 1072 #uuid "66e036d6-4c2a-53ff-a782-6b806cf9fd1c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284532 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 2t+(-2)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 2t-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{(2)}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2(2)-1⟩=⟨5\\,,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(5,3).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284217 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284533 1069 "q-164836" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284533 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-8)\\,i-4t\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284533 1072 #uuid "e59e7b4c-f2b2-52bd-8897-e35a51c56df3" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284533 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-8)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdo