[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284056 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284537 1069 "q-164859" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284537 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,2,0).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284537 1072 #uuid "476ff359-d053-5b12-8e13-351e9dc83e70" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284537 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,0) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 0-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5+\\frac{1}{2}(-6) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284078 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284538 1069 "q-165038" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284538 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284538 1072 #uuid "5a127c57-e392-5180-8b85-86041bead0c2" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284538 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284196 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284539 1069 "q-165040" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284539 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284539 1072 #uuid "41750cbe-5e6f-57d4-bbba-88d4814fe1e5" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284539 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283963 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284540 1069 "q-165041" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284540 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4}+4t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-2t\\,,\\,2{t}^{3}-3t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284540 1072 #uuid "001e493a-c5af-5f11-9758-cd2ce7d048b6" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284540 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}+4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{3}+4=0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2=c \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-3=3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=-1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=1.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{4}+4(-1)\\,,\\,(-1)^{3}-2(-1)\\,,\\,2(-1)^{3}-3(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(-3,1,1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284364 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284541 1069 "q-165042" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284541 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284541 1072 #uuid "c1d5d096-073b-5c49-80fa-a124c3af6414" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284541 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284283 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284542 1069 "q-165046" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284542 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t}\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2t+2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284542 1072 #uuid "fc3f1136-302e-5c6a-a6a1-4ab2d796baa7" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284542 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =c\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3}+5,\\,2t+2⟩ & =⟨\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,3c{t}^{2},\\,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}=\\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ {t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t+2=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2t.$ Substituting into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+2 & =2\\cdot (2t) \\\\ 2t+2 & =4t \\\\ 2t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the second equation ${t}^{3}+5=3c{t}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$ These values also satisfy the second equation. \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{1}\\,,\\,{1}^{3}+5\\,,\\,2(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,6,\\,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,6,4).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284036 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284543 1069 "q-165242" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284543 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j.$*" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284543 1072 #uuid "89e97ea8-0228-51af-89be-25d746d0bdd9" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284543 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,4t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(4t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(5{t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{(2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{8\\sqrt{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284253 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284544 1069 "q-165244" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284544 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284544 1072 #uuid "ddc00eac-51aa-5d12-b01c-079fe9ee6ad8" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284544 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${t}^{2}+1>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284011 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284545 1069 "q-165282" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284545 1071 "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284545 1072 #uuid "b54313ba-f7d3-5825-b960-2ab13d62a75d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284545 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284261 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284546 1069 "q-165297" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284546 1071 "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284546 1072 #uuid "e766e306-bf27-540a-8382-0386b59b504f" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284546 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284131 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284547 1069 "q-165299" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284547 1071 "The point $K$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $K?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284547 1072 #uuid "17b3eea6-c61f-5eb4-a4fa-c888e2a45ea7" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284547 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284150 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284548 1069 "q-165301" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284548 1071 "![](images/0f/0f89beaaf82692e398a3259140b735feb925395c6e6d9f63b72a5406d0d5073f.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $Q?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284548 1072 #uuid "1032bdd6-a90d-569e-8f67-11c90a4a9e5c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284548 1074 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284106 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284549 1069 "q-165340" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284549 1071 "![](images/57/57ce4048bd9357072fadcee7fb65c5e9c2823572ec48b8bb04fbaad3c464aa3d.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $Q?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284549 1072 #uuid "c97175a6-a15a-5191-b7ca-feec9c0939bf" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284549 1074 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $3$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284369 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284550 1069 "q-165413" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284550 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284550 1072 #uuid "47feb8ed-9de6-5cab-9530-5d8b3448a054" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284550 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284370 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284551 1069 "q-165414" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284551 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284551 1072 #uuid "e623a86a-8a2d-5ca1-949a-2b47126336d8" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284551 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284349 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284552 1069 "q-165420" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284552 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x}\\,i+\\frac{z}{y}\\,j+k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284552 1072 #uuid "edb29249-bdac-52a8-97b3-2eb2d8b60f89" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284552 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,z=t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{z}{x}\\,i+\\frac{z}{y}\\,j+k \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}\\,i+t{e}^{-t}\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t{e}^{-t}\\,i+t{e}^{-t}\\,j+k)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+k) \\\\  & =t+t+1 \\\\  & =2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284210 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284553 1069 "q-165421" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284553 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\,i+\\sqrt{y}\\,j+z\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284553 1072 #uuid "d129aab5-1367-543d-8fc9-4be96ed8cd86" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284553 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t,\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{x}\\,i+\\sqrt{y}\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\sqrt{t}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\sqrt{t}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (i+j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{t}+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{t}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2\\sqrt{t}+2{t}^{3})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3/2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+8-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}+37}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284347 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284554 1069 "q-165433" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284554 1071 "![](images/61/616116b9e5cf137c526b11dc5ce1647cd8f9cbd3a652be6dade9a8769467c27c.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284554 1072 #uuid "a86ebeb5-18d1-5f84-a46f-2e3c446ad259" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284554 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,15)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,15)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y-15).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(6-15) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-(y-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =-(y-15) \\\\ 25 & =-y+15 \\\\ y & =15-25 \\\\ y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284317 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284555 1069 "q-165434" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284555 1071 "![](images/64/6450376de8f9f4c0c8f5b414d95de67839664eda4f2056376160caddef47ba1f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284555 1072 #uuid "fd1afd08-8a39-5c55-a110-fbcb1f5435e5" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284555 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(4,0),$ we substitute $x=4$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(4-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}=-2(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283983 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284556 1069 "q-165470" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284556 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284556 1072 #uuid "580ff267-1264-5ccc-b330-51094868bb67" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284556 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(2,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{4}(x-2)\\,\\,y+2=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x-2).\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283946 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284557 1069 "q-16548" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284557 1071 "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284557 1072 #uuid "4ac4adf2-972b-5792-b6e6-5844a5be5de5" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284557 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284063 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284558 1069 "q-165482" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284558 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284558 1072 #uuid "5dc3ef25-ecd7-5c57-8a7f-d09fad78e669" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284558 1074 "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-8y-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-{x}^{2}-4x-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-[{x}^{2}+4x]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-[(x+2)^{2}-4]-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-(x+2)^{2}+4-16 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-(x+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{16}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(-2,1),\\,a=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-1=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}(x+2).\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284125 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284559 1069 "q-165483" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284559 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola \n${y}^{2}-25{x}^{2}-4y+50x-46=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284559 1072 #uuid "eacee4ab-e9ac-5442-99e7-1a412be5ee04" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284559 1074 "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-25{x}^{2}-4y+50x-46 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y-25{x}^{2}+50x-46 & =0 \\\\ [{y}^{2}-4y]-25[{x}^{2}-2x]-46 & =0 \\\\ [(y-2)^{2}-4]-25[(x-1)^{2}-1]-46 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4-25(x-1)^{2}+25-46 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-25(x-1)^{2} & =46+4-25 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-25(x-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25}-(x-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(1,2),\\,a=\\sqrt{25}=5,\\,b=\\sqrt{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny-2=\\pm 5(x-1).\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284331 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284560 1069 "q-16552" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284560 1071 "Given that $\\frac{3p+2q\\text{i}}{2-3\\text{i}}=1+2\\text{i}$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284560 1072 #uuid "09bb95d5-58eb-5040-b1a9-985bda1b1f6f" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284560 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2-3\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3p+2q\\text{i}}{2-3\\text{i}} & =1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =(1+2\\text{i})(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =2-3\\text{i}+4\\text{i}-6{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =2+\\text{i}-6(-1) \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =8+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =8 \\\\ p & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =1 \\\\ q & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283967 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284561 1069 "q-16553" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284561 1071 "![](images/0e/0efd7cec177f27696620bba018055fb6c834823d3c13413b325b4d69095e07dd.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284561 1072 #uuid "6910717e-3c06-5400-8f4a-4be89ee6a847" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284561 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(4,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284016 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284562 1069 "q-16556" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284562 1071 "Given that $7-6\\text{i}=(4p-3q\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284562 1072 #uuid "dcfe0e55-c7ee-5333-8fb9-d885799b22b6" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284562 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $1+2\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-6\\text{i} & =(4p-3q\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{7-6\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}} & =4p-3q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(7-6\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})}{(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})} & =4p-3q\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{7-14\\text{i}-6\\text{i}+12{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} & =4p-3q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{-5-20\\text{i}}{1+4} & =4p-3q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{-5-20\\text{i}}{5} & =4p-3q\\text{i} \\\\ -1-4\\text{i} & =4p-3q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $-1=4p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-4=-3q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284102 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284563 1069 "q-16561" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284563 1071 "Determine the length of the minor axis of the ellipse whose equation is  $25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284563 1072 #uuid "5ed2b043-750a-5637-b0ae-a564424a079c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284563 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100 & =0 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}+4x]+4[{y}^{2}-10y]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+4[(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-4]+4[(y-5)^{2}-25]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =-100+100+100 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>2,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284280 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284564 1069 "q-16562" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284564 1071 "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(6,-4)$\n- The length of the major axis is $6$\n- The length of the minor axis is $4$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284564 1072 #uuid "0602fa5a-d2db-5bf8-9142-ab8253a6eb55" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284564 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-12x]+9[{y}^{2}+8y]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+9[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-36]+9[(y+4)^{2}-16]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =-144+144+144 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 6=12,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(6,-4).$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, statements II and III are false." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284013 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284565 1069 "q-16565" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284565 1071 "Determine the length of the major axis of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284565 1072 #uuid "beb4f1e9-9ce4-5e8f-a547-7adfbf588a59" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284565 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0 \\\\ [{x}^{2}+10x]+4[{y}^{2}-4y]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+4[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-25]+4[(y-2)^{2}-4]+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =25-25+16 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 4=8.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283991 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284566 1069 "q-16566" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284566 1071 "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is\n$4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-16x+2y+13=0.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284566 1072 #uuid "59b2b2f3-1020-59f6-8e6c-939fc6a04444" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284566 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-16x+2y+13 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-16x)+({y}^{2}+2y)+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-16x)+({y}^{2}+2y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-4x]+[{y}^{2}+2y]+13 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+[(y+1)^{2}-{(1)}^{2}]+13 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-4]+[(y+1)^{2}-1]+13 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}-16+(y+1)^{2}-1+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-16+(y+1)^{2}-1+13 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =-13+1+16 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(2,-1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284178 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284567 1069 "q-165787" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284567 1071 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of $\\,{R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=2j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284567 1072 #uuid "4241c781-62cb-59f6-9f04-c94f40331fa0" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284567 1074 "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$ \n\n![](images/34/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=2j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/56/56a5f95d469cb63e20936a6c07971d9e80611d966282cb37d7ddf17fdc761bab.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284284 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284568 1069 "q-165971" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284568 1071 "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5x-2}+1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284568 1072 #uuid "f7bdbdc9-a156-56fb-bd3a-e5d66cea5150" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284568 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5(0)-2}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $5.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284285 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284569 1069 "q-165972" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284569 1071 "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={2}^{-4x+3}-5.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284569 1072 #uuid "3dd652cd-cbda-54cb-a604-be3b9cf6f944" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284569 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{-4(0)+3}-5 \\\\  & ={2}^{3}-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $3.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284030 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284570 1069 "q-165973" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284570 1071 "What is the range of $y=2(5)^{x}-3?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284570 1072 #uuid "e27217e2-3906-528c-a165-16295a0ecf55" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284570 1074 "The range of ${5}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then subtracting $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x}-3 & >0-3 \\\\ y & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in (-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x)=2(5)^{x}-3$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c8/c8179a12c8032d81ebbdb8a5a95c158e905fab4d24aa68f0eeef1f4bc4971a0b.png)" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284007 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284571 1069 "q-165974" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284571 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=2(5)^{x}-50.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284571 1072 #uuid "71eb07f1-c19e-5333-9a23-4af78cf8d610" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284571 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2(5)^{x}-50=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $50$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)^{x}-50 & =0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x}-\\cancel{50}+\\cancel{50} & =0+50 \\\\ 2(5)^{x} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)^{x} & =50 \\\\ {5}^{x} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $25$ as ${5}^{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284072 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284572 1069 "q-16639" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284572 1071 "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(0,0)$\n- The length of the major axis is $3$\n- The length of the minor axis is $2\\sqrt{3}$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284572 1072 #uuid "f98a559b-0a7a-50c5-b65c-b026bdee915b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284572 1074 "We rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3>\\sqrt{3},$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 3=6,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the ellipse is centered at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, statement II is false." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284049 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284573 1069 "q-16646" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284573 1071 "Calculate the points if any, where the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2}+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284573 1072 #uuid "b5699c1e-0ee8-5be1-a742-66538c4fc82b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284573 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(0-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(16) & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+32 & =-20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{52}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{52}{5}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283944 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284574 1069 "q-16647" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284574 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284574 1072 #uuid "ad41fa9c-427e-53d4-84ac-70d9cefddf98" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284574 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284234 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284575 1069 "q-166546" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284575 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)={log}_{5}⁡(2x+4)-1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284575 1072 #uuid "b3d8dc28-6054-520c-8968-9399a2a4230c" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284575 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284168 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284576 1069 "q-166549" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284576 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284576 1072 #uuid "b15a7e63-b8ab-5083-9941-85a35f55da3d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284576 1074 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4-2x) & =-1 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)} & ={2}^{-1} \\\\ 4-2x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2x & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{7}{4}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284365 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284577 1069 "q-166550" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284577 1071 "Find the minimum value of $f(x)=4{log}_{2}⁡(2x-3)+1.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284577 1072 #uuid "92f6c1a4-5622-588c-a7ee-2153dc26416b" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284577 1074 "The range of the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no minimum value." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284305 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284578 1069 "q-166585" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284578 1071 "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n7,\\,4,\\,11,\\,2,\\,6\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284578 1072 #uuid "3329c74b-da16-5511-b8b5-cdfee23df111" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284578 1074 "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (7+4+11+2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ({7}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (49+16+121+4+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{226}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{226}{5}-{(6)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{226}{5}-36 \\\\  & =\\frac{226-180}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{46}{5}}.\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284376 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284579 1069 "q-166602" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284579 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{-3}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284579 1072 #uuid "5525f3f9-de37-524f-895a-dbf6bcc144ca" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284579 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-3}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{4/3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{z}^{1/3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284312 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284580 1069 "q-16686" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284580 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=24$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284580 1072 #uuid "214063a9-14da-5222-9f49-e6768696ba83" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284580 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot {0}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{6}).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284175 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284581 1069 "q-167153" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284581 1071 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{-y}+y{e}^{z}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284581 1072 #uuid "5bb0fc77-ef03-5d09-9484-1c0a41da751d" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284581 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{e}^{-y}]\\,\\text{d}x+[-x{e}^{-y}+{e}^{z}]\\,\\text{d}y+[y{e}^{z}]\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}x+({e}^{z}-x{e}^{-y})\\,\\text{d}y+y{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284372 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284582 1069 "q-167160" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284582 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+2y-1$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1),$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284582 1072 #uuid "ddfa127f-a279-50f9-9daa-00b98d8bc9d1" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284582 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/81/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & ={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =1-0+0-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1-1+2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =0-1+2-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284324 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284583 1069 "q-167166" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284583 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{2y-z},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284583 1072 #uuid "7d3c9249-0c86-56ad-b2f3-cb8e23eff378" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284583 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}x+2x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}y-x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(-1,2,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(-1,2,3) & ={e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}y-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =e\\,\\text{d}x-2e\\,\\text{d}y+e\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(-1+Δx,2+Δy,3+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-(-1)=2, \\\\ Δy & =0-2=-2 \\\\ Δz & =1-3=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈e\\cdot (2)-2e\\cdot (-2)+e\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ≈4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $4e.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284174 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284584 1069 "q-167168" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284584 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284584 1072 #uuid "7ec3ee1a-9141-5963-a032-74283a58c8bc" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284584 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y+\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,1,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,1,1) & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+1\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,1+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈Δx+Δy+Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\\\ Δy & =3-1=2 \\\\ Δz & =4-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈Δx+Δy+Δz \\\\  & ≈1+2+3 \\\\  & ≈6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $6.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284373 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284585 1069 "q-167169" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284585 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=x+y-xy$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284585 1072 #uuid "481b8aaf-ce9a-528b-9903-76b0577c8546" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284585 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-y & 1-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-y=0 \\\\ 1-x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =t+0-t(0) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t⟨1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,t) \\\\  & =t+t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2t-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=1$ does not belong to the range $t\\in (0,1),$ we do not include the corresponding point in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =1+t-t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total, we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284098 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284586 1069 "q-167170" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284586 1071 "Estimate $\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}$ at the point $(0,0).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284586 1072 #uuid "862d8f4a-ef1a-5220-a521-4d8cdae9c576" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284586 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}=f(0.2,0.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.2,0.1).$\n\nMoving from $(0,0)$ to $(0.2,0.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.2-0=0.2, \\\\ Δy & =0.1-0=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)+\\frac{1}{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.15.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.2,0.1) & ≈f(0,0)+0.15 \\\\ \\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1} & ≈\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}+0.15 \\\\  & =1+0.15 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284247 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284587 1069 "q-167171" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284587 1071 "Estimate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)$ at the point $({\\pi }^{2},0).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284587 1072 #uuid "9eb149a3-bdcc-552e-a682-ced670cd4013" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284587 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,Δx-\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & ≈f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284340 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284588 1069 "q-167191" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284588 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{3}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284588 1072 #uuid "4fa5cea1-e99d-5174-a5ab-a853a89939e7" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284588 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3{z}^{2/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{3{z}^{2/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{z}^{2/3}}-{x}^{2}{z}^{1/3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nDoing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}z & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284180 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284589 1069 "q-167338" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284589 1071 "![](images/ec/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284589 1072 #uuid "9f5ee281-743c-52e5-8ba6-b17dfb13fd85" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284589 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284144 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284590 1069 "q-167453" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284590 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\,j+t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284590 1072 #uuid "78b6efe3-1fb5-5655-97e2-03def53db36e" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284590 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284035 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284591 1069 "q-167497" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284591 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284591 1072 #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284591 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \