=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284311 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284593 1069 "q-167499" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284593 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284593 1072 #uuid "b00d973d-5785-5a66-98c1-0a412869e903" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284593 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284094 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284594 1069 "q-167507" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284594 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284594 1072 #uuid "44bca005-fdff-5b04-a1fa-111c8c02f429" 13194139533406 17592186045585 true] [17592186284594 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t},